<p>Israel&#x27;s military said it has established a &quot;Yellow Line&quot; in southern Lebanon, calling it a boundary meant to keep people away from Israeli forces. The announcement appears to be the first time Israel has publicly used the term &quot;Yellow Line&quot; for Lebanon, after using similar language for areas in Gaza. Israeli statements said troops had struck people it identified as fighters in several areas and were still authorized to act against what it called threats near its positions. Reports also cited Israeli claims that operations targeted positions and tunnels linked to Hezbollah.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/GUJKgY18qEUSQiL1_2c9gnWsK2e0hqpA8VOMUYQkEOReW2HhyeNcWGkt5VZDLLqJOICVrAj9UtdUAmV_b-7EDBF7ppS3X4MnWLO8TpkEctm3bDYkl8LPTPYvYeAr6jERu1UyISds_2lp35JgYPCo34yLoE0wyltqECWNikVQHhCTVzODXBJEQZS9RHw_8s-GDHH3kgBRdJyU' alt='Image accompanying coverage of Israel&#x27;s announcement of a &#x27;Yellow Line&#x27; in southern Lebanon' /><br /><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Israeli military: It said the new line is meant to prevent people from approaching its forces and that troops are authorized to strike what it defines as threats in southern Lebanon. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/israel-army-says-established-yellow-line-in-lebanon/article70878638.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera: Its report highlighted the Lebanon announcement because Israel had previously used the same &quot;yellow line&quot; framing in Gaza, making this a new public label in the Lebanon context. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/18/israel-says-established-a-yellow-line-in-lebanon-as-it-has-in-gaza'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>AFP via The Korea Herald: The report described the move as part of ongoing Israeli enforcement after ceasefire arrangements, including strikes on people approaching its forces and on infrastructure Israel says is tied to Hezbollah. (<a href='https://www.koreaherald.com/article/10720362'>The Korea Herald</a>)</li></ul>